><text color="#9643FC" creationdate="D:20250626122832+10'00'" flags="print,nozoom,norotate" inreplyto="20df8215-5490-43d0-b664-321982787472" date="D:20250626122832+10'00'" name="c576a9cc-8f75-454b-ab83-a1643763aecc" icon="Comment" page="8" rect="35.655289,684.488159,59.655289,708.488159" subject="Sticky Note" title="alexd"
><contents-richtext
><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:25.1.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2"
><p dir="ltr"
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-14.0pt;text-align:left;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Q14c: </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-13.3pt;text-align:left;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Repeated-sprint exercise in the heat induced increases in plasma&#xD;[I-FABP], [LBP], [sCD14], and [HSP72], and yielded greater thermal,&#xD;physiological, and perceptual strain compared with exercise in&#xD;the COOL. Following 5 days of repeated-sprinting, this response was&#xD;partly maintained with [I-FABP] and [sCD14] increasing after exercise,&#xD;and [LBP] increasing only in the cool condition. In contrast,&#xD;plasma [HSP72] was decreased following the training regimen in the&#xD;heat, suggestive of emerging thermal tolerance. These findings indicate&#xD;that heat stress exacerbates gastrointestinal damage when performing&#xD;repeated sprints, although it appears to be mild. Moreover,&#xD;despite the emergence of thermoregulatory adaptations, perceptual&#xD;improvements and thermal tolerance, a greater thermal stimulus, or&#xD;extended protocol, appear required to induce changes in the GI&#xD;epithelium that lead to attenuated damage and permeability.</span
></p
></body
></contents-richtext
><popup flags="print,nozoom,norotate" open="no" page="8" rect="612.000000,616.488159,796.000000,708.488159"
/></text
></annots
